(* Q1: State Dirichlet's conditions for the existence of a Fourier series of a) Tj
T*
(function f\(x\) in \(-pi, pi\).) Tj
T*
(Answer: Dirichlet's conditions guarantee that a function f\(x\) defined on \(-pi, pi\) has a) Tj
T*
(convergent Fourier series: \(1\) f\(x\) must be single-valued and bounded everywhere in) Tj
T*
(\(-pi, pi\). \(2\) f\(x\) must have at most a finite number of jump discontinuities within any) Tj
T*
(single period. \(3\) f\(x\) must have at most a finite number of local maxima and local) Tj
T*
(minima in that interval. When these conditions hold, the series converges to f\(x\) at) Tj
T*
(every point of continuity, and converges to [f\(x+\) + f\(x-\)] / 2 at any point of) Tj
T*
(discontinuity.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain Parseval's identity for Fourier series and its application in summing) Tj
T*
(infinite numerical series.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Parseval's identity equates the mean-square value of a periodic function to the) Tj
T*
(sum of the squares of its Fourier coefficients: \(1 / 2L\) * int_{-L}^L [f\(x\)]^2 dx = \(a0) Tj
T*
(/ 2\)^2 + \(1 / 2\) * sum_{n=1}^inf \(an^2 + bn^2\). In the interval \(-pi, pi\), this becomes:) Tj
T*
(\(1 / pi\) * int_{-pi}^{pi} [f\(x\)]^2 dx = a0^2 / 2 + sum_{n=1}^inf \(an^2 + bn^2\).) Tj
T*
(Parseval's theorem is extraordinarily powerful in computing higher-order infinite) Tj
T*
(numerical series \(such as sum 1/n^4 or sum 1/n^6\) by simply evaluating the definite) Tj
T*
(integral of [f\(x\)]^2.) Tj
